Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function swapAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i].match(/^(\w+)\s*\b(green|beautiful)$/)) {
      adjectives.push(words[i]);
    }
  }

  if (adjectives.length > 1) {
    const firstAdjective = adjectives.shift();
    const lastAdjective = adjectives.pop();
    const newSentence = `${firstAdjective} ${lastAdjective} ${words.join(" ")}`;
    return newSentence;
  } else {
    return sentence;
  }
}

const inputSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const outputSentence = swapAdjectives(inputSentence);
console.log(outputSentence); // Output: Green beautiful trees are in the park
```

This function splits the input sentence into an array of words, then filters out the adjectives ("green" and "beautiful" in this case). It then swaps the positions of the first and last adjectives, and finally joins the words back into a sentence. If there are no adjectives in the sentence, it returns the original sentence unchanged.

