Your provided MATLAB code is a good starting point to find unique words in Text1 that do not appear in Text2. Here's an optimized version of the code that can handle a large amount of text more efficiently:

```matlab
function unique_words = find_unique_words(text1, text2)
    % Convert text to lowercase and split into words
    words1 = lower(strsplit(text1));
    words2 = lower(strsplit(text2));

    % Create a set data structure to store unique words
    unique_set = cellfun(@isempty, unique(words1), 'UniformOutput', false);

    % Iterate through words2 and check if they are in words1
    for i = 1:length(words2)
        if ~ismember(words2{i}, words1)
            unique_set{i} = true;
        end
    end

    % Convert the set data structure back to a cell array
    unique_words = cellfun(@(x) x{end}, unique_set, 'UniformOutput', false);
end
```

This function takes two input arguments: `text1` and `text2`. It first converts both texts to lowercase and splits them into words. Then, it creates a set data structure to store unique words. The `cellfun` function is used to iterate through `words2` and check if each word is in `words1`. If a word is not in `words1`, it is added to the unique set. Finally, the set data structure is converted back to a cell array and returned as the output.

To use this function, you can call it with your text files as arguments:

```matlab
