Here's an example of Google Apps Script that searches for a place name in a column and stores the latitude and longitude in other columns:

```javascript
function searchPlace() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSheet();
  var dataRange = sheet.getRange("A2:A"); // Assuming your data starts in cell A2
  var placeName = dataRange.getValue();

  var geocoder = new google.maps.Geocoder();
  geocoder.geocode({ address: placeName }, function(results, status) {
    if (status == 'OK') {
      var latitude = results[0].geometry.location.lat();
      var longitude = results[0].geometry.location.lng();

      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue(latitude); // Assuming you want to store the latitude in column B
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue(longitude); // Assuming you want to store the longitude in column C
    } else {
      console.log('Geocode was unsuccessful for the following reason: ' + status);
    }
  });
}
```

To use this script, follow these steps:

1. Open your Google Sheet.
2. Click on "Extensions" > "Apps Script."
3. Copy and paste the code above into the script editor.
4. Save the script.
5. Go back to your Google Sheet.
6. Click on the cell where you want to search for the place name (e.g., cell A2).
7. Click on the "Search Place" button in the script editor.
8. The script will search for the place name in the selected cell and store the latitude and longitude in the corresponding cells (B2 and C2 in this example).

Note: You'll need to enable the Google Maps API for your script by going to "Resources" > "Advanced Google services" and enabling the "Google Maps JavaScript API."

Here's a PHP custom function "smart_sort_array" that meets your requirements:

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order) {
    usort($set, function ($a, $b) use ($order) {
        return $order === 'asc' ? strcmp($a['age'], $b['age']) : strcmp($b['age'], $a['age']);
    });

    return $set;
}
```

To use this function, simply call it with an array as the first argument and either 'asc' or 'desc' as the second argument:

