];

$sorted_set = smart_sort_array($set, 'asc'); // Sort in ascending order
$sorted_set = smart_sort_array($set, 'desc'); // Sort in descending order

print_r($sorted_set);
```

This will output the sorted set in either ascending or descending order based on the value of the 'order' parameter.

Here's a JavaScript function that takes a string variable in dot notation and returns all entries in an option where the nested key value matches the string key:

```javascript
function getEntriesByKey(object, key) {
  const entries = [];
  Object.entries(object).forEach(([nestedKey, value]) => {
    if (typeof value === 'object') {
      const result = getEntriesByKey(value, key);
      if (result.length > 0) {
        entries.push({ ...result[0], nestedKey });
      }
    } else if (nestedKey === key) {
      entries.push({ value, nestedKey });
    }
  });
  return entries;
}

// Example usage
const object = {
  a: {
    b: {
      c: 1,
    },
    d: 2,
  },
  e: 3,
};

const key = 'c';

const entries = getEntriesByKey(object, key);
console.log(entries); // Output: [{ value: 1, nestedKey: 'a.b.c' }]
```

This function uses recursion to traverse the object and find all entries where the nested key value matches the given string key. The function returns an array of objects, each with the matched entry and its corresponding nested key.

