Inner and outer functions refer to the concept of function composition in functional programming. Inner functions are functions that are defined within another function, while outer functions are functions that are defined outside of any other function.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

```javascript
// Outer function
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

// Inner function
function multiply(a, b) {
  return a * b;
}

// Composition of inner and outer functions
const composedFunction = add(multiply(2, 3)); // Returns 14
```

In this example, `add` is an outer function that takes two arguments (`a` and `b`) and returns their sum. `multiply` is an inner function that also takes two arguments (`a` and `b`) and returns their product. The `composedFunction` is created by composing `add` and `multiply`, which means applying `multiply` to the arguments of `add`.

The key difference between inner and outer functions is that inner functions have access to the lexical scope of their outer function, meaning they can access variables and functions defined in the outer function. On the other hand, outer functions do not have access to the lexical scope of their inner functions.

Inner functions are often used to create reusable code patterns or to encapsulate complex logic. They can also be used to implement higher-order functions, which are functions that take other functions as arguments or return functions.

Outer functions are often used to define standalone functions that can be called from other parts of the code. They can also be used to create modules or libraries that provide a set of related functions.

In summary, inner and outer functions are concepts related to function composition in functional programming. Inner functions are defined within another function and have access to the lexical scope of their outer function, while outer functions are defined outside of any other function and do not have access to the lexical scope of their inner functions. Both types of functions have their own use cases and can be used to create powerful and reusable code patterns.

