The given MATLAB code calculates the distance between two points on the Earth's surface considering it as an ellipsoid. To find the distance between San Francisco and London, we need to input the corresponding latitudes and longitudes for these two cities.

Here's the modified code with the latitudes and longitudes of San Francisco and London:

```matlab
% Earth's semi-major axis in meters
a = 6378137;

% Earth's semi-minor axis in meters
b = 6356752.3142;

% Latitude of San Francisco in degrees
lat1 = deg2rad(37.7749);

% Longitude of San Francisco in degrees
lon1 = deg2rad(-122.4194);

% Latitude of London in degrees
lat2 = deg2rad(51.5074);

% Longitude of London in degrees
lon2 = deg2rad(-0.1278);

% Earth's flattening
f = (a-b)/a;

% Earth's eccentricity squared
e2 = 2*f - f^2;

% Radius of curvature in the prime vertical at point 1
N1 = a/sqrt(1-e2*sin(lat1)^2);

% Radius of curvature in the prime vertical at point 2
N2 = a/sqrt(1-e2*sin(lat2)^2);

% Cartesian coordinates of point 1
x1 = (N1+h1)*cos(lat1)*cos(lon1);
y1 = (N1+h1)*cos(lat1)*sin(lon1);
z1 = ((1-e2)*N1+h1)*sin(lat1);

% Cartesian coordinates of point 2
