A logical syllogism is a type of argument that consists of two premises and a conclusion, where the conclusion logically follows from the premises. Here's an example of a logical syllogism:

Premise 1: All humans are mortal.
Premise 2: Socrates is human.
Conclusion: Socrates is mortal.

This syllogism works by using the first premise to establish a general rule about all humans (they are mortal), and then using the second premise to identify a specific individual (Socrates) who falls under that rule. Since Socrates is human, and all humans are mortal, it follows that Socrates must also be mortal.

Here's the same syllogism formatted using LaTeX:

\begin{syllogism}
    \premise{All humans are mortal.}
    \premise{Socrates is human.}
    \conclusion{Socrates is mortal.}
\end{syllogism}

