Here's an R code snippet that checks if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric:

```R
# Function to check if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric
is_all_numeric <- function(x) {
  is_numeric <- function(x) {
    tryCatch({
      as.numeric(x)
      TRUE
    }, error = function(e) FALSE)
  }

  all(is_numeric(x[!is.na(x)]))
}

# Example usage
x <- c("1", "2", "3", "4", NA, "5")
print(is_all_numeric(x)) # Output: FALSE

y <- c("1", "2", "3", "4", "5")
print(is_all_numeric(y)) # Output: TRUE
```

This code defines a function `is_all_numeric` that takes a character vector `x` as input and returns a logical value indicating whether all non-NA elements in `x` can be converted to numeric. The function uses a nested `tryCatch` block to handle any errors that may occur during the conversion process. If all elements can be converted to numeric without any errors, the function returns `TRUE`, otherwise it returns `FALSE`.

